Title:
pool-proxy-ng: Clean Call Stacks Through the Thread Pool

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post describes a rewrite of PoolProxy (“pool-proxy-ng”), a library that proxies Windows API calls through the Windows thread pool to keep implant code addresses off the call stack during sensitive API execution.  
- It solves the common EDR stack-walking IOC where a return address points into suspicious/unbacked memory by ensuring execution appears to originate from thread pool dispatcher frames and returns into a signed-module gadget.  
- The rewrite adds multi-gadget/multi-register support, increases maximum arguments (up to 15), removes a return-value race condition via `TpWaitForWork`, and implements automatic gadget discovery/verification across OS builds.  
- A key addition is handling “pop-chain” gadgets by encoding register-restore layouts in a compact `popMap` and having the trampoline pre-fill stack slots so callee-saved registers are restored correctly.  
- It also introduces “side-effect DLL loading” (loading gadget-hosting DLLs via legitimate dependency chains) to avoid obvious `LoadLibrary` call stacks during initialization.  
- Useful primarily for red teams, implant developers, and offensive security researchers focused on stealthy user-mode execution; it also highlights CET (shadow stack) as a looming constraint for this technique.

Technical Focus:
- Windows thread pool callback execution (`TppWorkpExecuteCallback`) for stack camouflage  
- ROP-style return-value capture gadgets (`mov [reg], rax; add rsp, N; ...; ret`)  
- Pop-chain aware stack layout construction (`popMap` encoding, callee-saved register restoration)  
- Gadget verification and micro-scanning across DLL versions/builds  
- Side-effect DLL loading via COM / API dependency chains  
- CET / shadow stack incompatibility with `push gadget; jmp target` dispatch

Use Cases:
- Proxying high-signal WinAPI/NtAPI calls (e.g., `NtCreateFile`, injection-related APIs) while avoiding implant return addresses on the stack
- Building stealthier loaders/implants that execute from suspicious memory regions without stack-walk IOCs
- Researching and testing EDR stack-walking detections and their limitations
- Developing gadget databases and runtime gadget selection/verification for user-mode tradecraft
- Exploring CET-aware alternatives (`NtContinue`, `RtlRestoreContext`, forward-edge compliant dispatch)

Keywords:
Windows thread pool, TppWorkpExecuteCallback, call stack spoofing, stack walking, EDR evasion, ROP gadget, mov [reg], rax, callee-saved registers, pop chain, RtlPcToFileHeader, TpWaitForWork, NtCreateFile, gadget scanning, signed DLL, side-effect DLL loading, CoCreateInstance, PEB module list, CET, shadow stack, IMAGE_DLLCHARACTERISTICS_EX_CET_COMPAT